// Phase 2 gate 2.3: a concurrent producer/consumer over a bounded channel,
// with timers, producing deterministic output.
//
// Determinism comes from one decision: only the consumer prints. The channel
// is FIFO, so the order of `got` lines is fixed by the order of sends no
// matter how the two tasks interleave, and the sleep changes only *when* each
// value moves -- never which. If both tasks printed, the interleaving would
// vary with timing and this example would falsify the very property the gate
// asks it to demonstrate.
//
// So the sleep here *demonstrates* that channels and timers compose; it is not
// what the assertion rests on. This suite has a standing rule against
// timing-flaky assertions -- see `gate_task_sleep_order_runs` -- and nothing
// below asserts a duration.
//
// Capacity 2 against 5 sends is deliberate: the producer fills the buffer and
// blocks in `send` twice, so this exercises the bounded path rather than the
// case where a channel never fills. `tx.close()` is what ends the consumer's
// loop -- `recv` returns `None` only once the channel is closed *and* drained,
// so without that call the consumer would wait forever.
//
// `let ch: Channel<Int>` needs its annotation: `channel`'s type parameter
// appears only in the return type, and Nova has no turbofish, so `channel(2)`
// alone gives the checker no way to name `T`.

async fn produce(mut tx: Sender<Int>) {
    let mut n = 1
    while n <= 5 {
        tx.send(n).await
        sleep(Duration::from_millis(1)).await
        n = n + 1
    }
    tx.close()
}

async fn consume(mut rx: Receiver<Int>) {
    let mut more = true
    while more {
        match rx.recv().await {
            Some(v) => println("got ${v}")
            None => more = false
        }
    }
    println("done")
}

async fn main() {
    let ch: Channel<Int> = channel(2)
    let a = spawn(produce(ch.sender()))
    let b = spawn(consume(ch.receiver()))
    a.join().await
    b.join().await
}
